Based on the provided code fragments, I can see that the Hexi library uses prefix-based string serialization. The `prefixed` and `prefixed_varint` adaptors are used for writing and reading strings with length prefixes.

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <string>

void write_string_to_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, const std::string& str) {
    writer << hexi::prefixed(str);
}

std::string read_string_from_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader) {
    std::string result;
    reader >> hexi::prefixed(result);
    return result;
}
```

Alternatively, if you prefer to use `prefixed_varint` (which uses variable-length integer encoding for the prefix), you could implement it like this:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <string>

void write_string_to_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, const std::string& str) {
    writer << hexi::prefixed_varint(str);
}

std::string read_string_from_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader) {
    std::string result;
    reader >> hexi::prefixed_varint(result);
    return result;
}
```

The main difference is:
- `prefixed` uses a fixed-size prefix (typically 4 bytes for the length)
- `prefixed_varint` uses a variable-length integer encoding for the prefix, which can be more space-efficient for short strings

Based on the code fragments showing `operator>>(std::string& data)` calling `prefixed(data)`, I believe the first implementation using `prefixed` is the intended approach.

