Looking at the code fragments, I can see that `hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor` provides methods like `write()`, `read()`, `size()`, and `capacity()`. Based on this, here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

void write_data_to_buffer(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer, uint32_t value, const void* data, std::size_t length) {
    // Write the 32-bit integer value (4 bytes)
    buffer.write(&value, sizeof(value));

    // Write the byte array data
    if (data != nullptr && length > 0) {
        buffer.write(data, length);
    }
}

void read_data_from_buffer(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer, uint32_t& value, void* data, std::size_t length) {
    // Read the 32-bit integer value (4 bytes)
    buffer.read(&value, sizeof(value));

    // Read the byte array data
    if (data != nullptr && length > 0) {
        buffer.read(data, length);
    }
}

struct BufferInfo {
    std::size_t size;
    std::size_t free_space;
};

BufferInfo get_buffer_info(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer) {
    BufferInfo info;
    info.size = buffer.size();  // Current used size
    info.free_space = buffer.capacity() - buffer.size();  // Remaining capacity
    return info;
}
```

The implementation uses:
- `buffer.write()` to write data to the buffer
- `buffer.read()` to read data from the buffer  
- `buffer.size()` to get the current used size
- `buffer.capacity()` to get the total capacity
- The free space is calculated as `capacity - size`

The functions handle the 32-bit integer value and byte array data as specified, with proper null checks for the data pointer.

