Looking at the code fragments, I can see that `buffer_adaptor` has a `copy()` method that copies data without advancing the read pointer, and a `read_ptr()` method that returns a pointer to the current read position.

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>

void peek_data_from_buffer(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer, void* data, std::size_t length) {
    // Use the copy method to copy data without advancing read pointer
    buffer.copy(data, length);
}

template<typename T>
void peek_value_from_buffer(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer, T& value) {
    // Use the template version of copy for single values
    buffer.copy(&value, sizeof(T));
}

bool peek_and_compare(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer, const void* expected, std::size_t length) {
    // Get pointer to current read position
    const std::byte* read_ptr = buffer.read_ptr();

    // Compare the data at the read position with expected data
    return std::memcmp(read_ptr, expected, length) == 0;
}
```

The implementation uses:
1. `buffer.copy(data, length)` for the byte array version - this copies data without advancing the read pointer
2. `buffer.copy(&value, sizeof(T))` for the template version - this copies a single value of type T
3. `buffer.read_ptr()` to get a pointer to the current read position, then `memcmp` to compare with expected data

All operations preserve the read pointer position as required for "peek" functionality.

