Looking at the code fragments and the requirements, I need to implement two functions for creating and verifying filled arrays. The `create_filled_array` function should generate a compile-time constant array filled with a specific value, and `verify_filled_array` should check if all elements in an array match the expected value.

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/shared.h>
#include <array>
#include <cstdint>

using namespace hexi::detail;

template<std::size_t Size>
constexpr auto create_filled_array(std::uint8_t value) {
    // Create an array and fill it with the specified value at compile time
    std::array<std::uint8_t, Size> arr{};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < Size; ++i) {
        arr[i] = value;
    }
    return arr;
}

bool verify_filled_array(const auto& arr, std::uint8_t expected_value) {
    // Verify all elements equal expected_value
    for (const auto& element : arr) {
        if (element != expected_value) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
```

The implementation:

1. **`create_filled_array`**: 
   - Creates a `std::array<std::uint8_t, Size>` at compile time using `constexpr`
   - Uses a simple loop to fill each element with the specified value
   - Returns the filled array

2. **`verify_filled_array`**:
   - Uses a range-based for loop to iterate through all elements
   - Returns `false` immediately if any element doesn't match the expected value
   - Returns `true` only if all elements match the expected value

This implementation is efficient and meets the requirements for compile-time array generation and runtime verification.

