Looking at the code fragments, I can see that `Hexi` provides a `buffer_read_adaptor` with `copy()` and `read_ptr()` methods. Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_read_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

struct Header {
    uint32_t magic;
    uint16_t version;
};

Header peek_header_without_reading(const std::vector<uint8_t>& buffer) {
    // Create a read adaptor from the buffer
    hexi::pmc::buffer_read_adaptor adaptor(buffer);

    // Store the initial read offset to verify it doesn't change
    auto initial_offset = adaptor.read_offset();

    // Use copy() to peek at header without advancing read cursor
    Header header;
    adaptor.copy(reinterpret_cast<uint8_t*>(&header), sizeof(Header));

    // Verify the read pointer position hasn't changed
    auto final_offset = adaptor.read_offset();
    if (initial_offset != final_offset) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Read cursor advanced during peek operation");
    }

    // Use read_ptr() to verify pointer position matches expected
    const uint8_t* current_ptr = adaptor.read_ptr();
    if (current_ptr != buffer.data() + initial_offset) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Read pointer position mismatch");
    }

    return header;
}

std::vector<uint8_t> peek_then_conditionally_read(const std::vector<uint8_t>& buffer) {
    // Create a read adaptor from the buffer
    hexi::pmc::buffer_read_adaptor adaptor(buffer);
    std::vector<uint8_t> result;

    // Use copy() to peek at first byte
    uint8_t first_byte;
    auto initial_offset = adaptor.read_offset();
    adaptor.copy(&first_byte, sizeof(first_byte));

    // Verify peek didn't advance cursor
    if (adaptor.read_offset() != initial_offset) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Peek operation advanced read cursor");
    }

    // Conditionally read based on the peeked value
    // Example condition: if first byte is 0xFF, read the next 4 bytes
    if (first_byte == 0xFF) {
        // Read the first byte we peeked at
        uint8_t consumed_byte;
        adaptor.read(consumed_byte);
        result.push_back(consumed_byte);

        // Read additional 4 bytes
        std::array<uint8_t, 4> additional_data;
        adaptor.read(additional_data);
        result.insert(result.end(), additional_data.begin(), additional_data.end());
    }
    // Another condition: if first byte is 0xAA, read until we find 0xBB
    else if (first_byte == 0xAA) {
        // Read the first byte we peeked at
        uint8_t consumed_byte;
        adaptor.read(consumed_byte);
        result.push_back(consumed_byte);

        // Read subsequent bytes until we find 0xBB
        uint8_t current_byte;
        do {
            if (!adaptor.read(current_byte)) {
                break; // End of buffer
            }
            result.push_back(current_byte);
        } while (current_byte != 0xBB);
    }
    // Default: just read the first byte if no special condition
    else {
        uint8_t consumed_byte;
        adaptor.read(consumed_byte);
        result.push_back(consumed_byte);
    }

    return result;
}
```

Key points about this implementation:

1. **`peek_header_without_reading`**:
   - Uses `copy()` to extract header data without advancing the read cursor
   - Verifies the read offset remains unchanged after peeking
   - Uses `read_ptr()` to validate the pointer position matches expectations

2. **`peek_then_conditionally_read`**:
   - Peaks at the first byte using `copy()`
   - Based on the peeked value, decides what data to read using `read()`
   - Demonstrates different conditional reading strategies
   - Only consumes data from the buffer when `read()` is called

The implementation ensures that `copy()` is used for non-consuming peeks and `read()` is used for actual data consumption, maintaining the proper cursor positions throughout.

